## 5.2 Headline composition

Inspect actual long headlines.

For every major headline, consider:

- Maximum line width.
- Line breaks.
- Optical centering.
- Weight.
- Leading.
- Letter spacing.
- Text balance.
- Relationship with the adjacent media.

Do not force an identical number of lines.

Do not use aggressive truncation for important headlines.

Ensure Chinese text wraps elegantly and never creates awkward isolated characters.

## 5.3 Body typography

Long-form text should be comfortable.

Improve:

- Reading measure.
- Line height.
- Paragraph spacing.
- Inline emphasis.
- List hierarchy.
- Citation spacing.
- Section heading transitions.

Maintain approximately 65–75ch for long-form reading, adjusted according to the actual font and language.

## 5.4 Metadata typography

Not every metadata value deserves monospace.

Use IBM Plex Mono selectively for:

- IDs.
- Dates.
- Tabular figures.
- Numerical values.
- Technical provenance details.

Use the sans-serif family for descriptive metadata.

Use stronger type contrast to distinguish important and secondary metadata.

Do not make crucial information visually tiny.

## 5.5 Chinese and English typography

The two languages must receive equal attention.

Audit the font stacks.

In particular, inspect the current `--font-mono` stack and its Chinese fallback behavior.

The existing documentation and actual CSS should agree.

Improve:

- CJK font fallback.
- Chinese heading proportions.
- Line height.
- Chinese punctuation.
- Mixed Chinese/English labels.
- Number alignment.
- Metadata widths.
- Paragraph rhythm.

Do not simply use the same typography values for every language.

